== Chapter III

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[The second general principle of the means of mortification set out to be confirmed — The Spirit the only author of this work — The emptiness of popish mortification exposed — Many of the means they use not appointed by God — Those appointed by him abused — The mistakes of others in this business — The Spirit is promised to believers for this work, Ezek 11:19; 36:26 — All that we receive from Christ is by the Spirit — How the Spirit mortifies sin — Gal 5:19–23 — The several ways he works to this end — How it is both his work and our duty.]

#smallcaps[The] next principle concerns the great sovereign cause of the mortification we are dealing with. In the words laid down as the foundation of this discourse, it is said to be the Spirit, that is, the Holy Spirit, as was shown.

II. #emph[He alone is sufficient for this work. All ways and means without him are worth nothing, and he is the great effective cause of it: he works in us as he pleases.]

1\. Men seek other remedies in vain; they will not be healed by them. It is well known how many ways have been prescribed for this, to have sin mortified. The greatest part of popish religion, of what looks most like religion in their profession, consists of mistaken ways and means of mortification. This is the pretense of their rough garments, by which they deceive. Their vows, orders, fastings, and penances are all built on this ground; they are all for the mortifying of sin. Their preaching, sermons, and books of devotion all look this way. That is why some interpret the locusts that came out of the bottomless pit (Rev 9:3) as the friars of the Roman church. These locusts are said to torment men, so that “men will seek death and will not find it” (verse 6). Those interpreters think the friars did it by their stinging sermons. By these they convinced men of sin, but being unable to show them the remedy for healing and mortifying it, they kept them in such constant anguish and terror, and such trouble of conscience, that they longed to die. This, I say, is the substance and glory of their religion. But they labor to mortify dead creatures, ignorant of the nature and end of the work, and they mix poison into it by their belief that it earns merit, yes, #emph[supererogation] (as they call their unnecessary merit, with a proud, barbarous title). So their glory is their shame. But more of them and their mortification later, in ch. 7.

It is well known that the ways and means for mortifying sin that they invented are still urged and prescribed, for the same end, by some who should have more light and knowledge of the gospel. Directions of this kind have lately been given by some, and eagerly snatched up by others who profess themselves Protestants, directions that would have suited popish devotees three or four hundred years ago. Such outward efforts, such bodily exercises, such self-performances, such merely legal duties, without the least mention of Christ or his Spirit, are varnished over with inflated, empty words as the only means and expedients for the mortification of sin. They reveal a deep-rooted unfamiliarity with the power of God and the mystery of the gospel. The thought of this was one motive for publishing this plain discourse.

Now, among other reasons, these are why the Papists, for all their efforts, can never truly mortify a single sin:

(1.) Many of the ways and means they use and insist on for this end were never appointed by God for that purpose. (Now, nothing in religion has any power to bring about an end except from God's appointing it to that purpose.) Such are their rough garments, their vows, penances, disciplines, their course of monastic life, and the like. Of all these God will say, “Who has required this of you#super[+]?” and, “They worship Me in vain; they teach as doctrine the precepts of men.” Of the same nature are various self-inflicted hardships urged by others.

(2.) The things that God has appointed as means are not used by them in their proper place and order: such as prayer, fasting, watching, meditation, and the like. These have their use in the business at hand. But while they are all to be regarded as streams, they regard them as the fountain. They bring about the end only as means, under the Spirit and faith; but they think these do it by the mere doing of the work. If they fast so much, and pray so much, and keep their hours and times, the work is done. As the apostle says of some in another case, they are “always learning but never able to come to a knowledge of the truth”; so these are always mortifying, but never come to any sound mortification. In a word, they have various means to mortify the natural man, as to the natural life we lead here, but none to mortify lust or corruption.

This is the general mistake of men ignorant of the gospel about this matter, and it lies at the bottom of a great deal of the superstition and will-worship that has been brought into the world. What horrible ways of wasting their own bodies some of the ancient founders of monastic devotion practiced! What violence they did to nature! What extremes of suffering they put themselves through! Search their ways and principles to the bottom, and you will find that it had no other root than this mistake: attempting rigid mortification, they fell upon the natural man instead of the corrupt old man, upon the body in which we live instead of the body of death.

Nor will the natural popery that is in others do it. Men are stung by the guilt of a sin that has prevailed over them. At once they promise themselves and God that they will do it no more. They watch over themselves and pray for a while, until this heat grows cold and the sense of sin wears off. Then mortification goes too, and sin returns to its former rule. Duties are excellent food for a healthy soul; they are no medicine for a sick soul. He who turns his food into his medicine must not expect it to do much. Spiritually sick men cannot sweat out their disease by working. But this is the way of men who deceive their own souls, as we shall see later.

That none of these ways is sufficient is clear from the nature of the work itself that has to be done. It is a work that requires so many things working together in it that no self-effort can reach it, and it is of such a kind that almighty power is needed to accomplish it, as will be shown later.

2\. It is, then, the work of the Spirit. For:

(1.) He is #emph[promised] by God to be given to us to do this work. The taking away of the heart of stone, that is, the stubborn, proud, rebellious, unbelieving heart, is in general the work of mortification we are dealing with. Now this is always promised to be done by the Spirit (Ezek 11:19; 36:26): “I will put My Spirit within you#super[+] … I will remove your#super[+] heart of stone.” And it is by the Spirit of God that this work is done when all other means fail (Isa 57:17, 18).

(2.) We have all our mortification from the #emph[gift] of Christ, and all the gifts of Christ are communicated and given to us by the Spirit of Christ: “Without Christ we can do nothing” (John 15:5). All supplies and relief, in the beginning, growth, and exercise of any grace whatever, come from him by the Spirit, by whom alone he works in and on believers. From him we have our mortification. He is “exalted … as Prince and Savior, in order to grant repentance” to us (Acts 5:31), and our mortification is no small part of our repentance. How does he do it? Having “received from the Father the promised Holy Spirit,” he sends him out for that end (Acts 2:33). You#super[+] know the many promises he made of sending the Spirit, “Vicariam navare operam,” as Tertullian puts it, to do the work he had to accomplish in us.

Answering one or two questions will now bring me closer to what I chiefly intend.

The first is, #emph[How does the Spirit mortify sin?]

I answer, in general, in three ways:

\[1.\] By causing our hearts to abound in #emph[grace] and the fruits that are contrary to the flesh, to its fruits, and to their principles. So the apostle sets the fruits of the flesh against the fruits of the Spirit. “The fruits of the flesh,” he says, “are such and such” (Gal 5:19–21); “but the fruits of the Spirit,” he says, “are quite contrary, of an entirely different kind” (verses 22, 23). Yes, but if these are in us and abound, may not the others abound too? No, he says (verse 24): “Those who belong to Christ Jesus have crucified the flesh with its passions and desires.” But how? Why, by “living in the Spirit and walking by the Spirit” (verse 25); that is, by these graces of the Spirit abounding in us, and by our walking according to them. For, says the apostle, “they are opposed to each other” (verse 17), so that they cannot both be in the same person in any intense or high degree. This “renewal by the Holy Spirit,” as it is called (Titus 3:5), is one great way of mortification. He causes us to grow, thrive, flourish, and abound in those graces which are contrary, opposite, and destructive to all the fruits of the flesh, and to the quiet or thriving of indwelling sin itself.

\[2.\] By a #emph[real, physical working] on the root and habit of sin, to weaken, destroy, and take it away. That is why he is called “a spirit of judgment and a spirit of fire” (Isa 4:4), really consuming and destroying our lusts. He takes away the heart of stone by almighty power; for as he begins the work in its kind, so he carries it on in its degrees. He is the fire that burns up the very root of lust.

\[3.\] He brings the #emph[cross of Christ] into the heart of a sinner by faith, and gives us communion with Christ in his death and fellowship in his sufferings. Of how he does this, more later.

Secondly. If this is the work of the Spirit alone, why are we exhorted to it? Since only the Spirit of God can do it, let the work be left wholly to him.

\[1.\] It is the work of the Spirit in no other way than all the graces and good works in us are his. He “works in you#super[+] to will and to act on behalf of His good purpose” (Phil 2:13). He has done for us “all that we have accomplished” (Isa 26:12), and he will “powerfully fulfill” the “work of faith” (2 Thess 1:11; Col 2:12). He causes us to pray, and is “a spirit of grace and prayer” (Rom 8:26; Zech 12:10). And yet we are exhorted, and are to be exhorted, to all these.

\[2.\] He does not work our mortification in us in such a way that it ceases to be an act of our #emph[obedience]. The Holy Spirit works in us and on us as we are fit to be worked in and on; that is, so as to preserve our own liberty and free obedience. He works on our understandings, wills, consciences, and affections in keeping with their own natures. He works #emph[in us] and #emph[with us], not #emph[against us] or #emph[without us]. So his help is an encouragement, making the work easier, and no excuse for neglecting the work itself. And indeed I might here lament the endless, foolish labor of poor souls who, convinced of sin and unable to stand against the power of their convictions, set themselves by countless perplexing ways and duties to keep sin down; but being strangers to the Spirit of God, they do it all in vain. They fight without victory, have war without peace, and are in slavery all their days. They spend their strength on what is not bread, and their labor on what does not satisfy.

This is the saddest warfare that any poor creature can be engaged in. A soul under the power of conviction from the law is pressed to fight against sin, but has no strength for the fight. They cannot help fighting, and they can never conquer. They are like men thrust onto the swords of their enemies on purpose to be slain. The #emph[law] drives them on, and sin beats them back. Sometimes, indeed, they think they have defeated sin, when they have only raised a dust so that they cannot see it. That is, they stir up their natural feelings of fear, sorrow, and anguish, and this makes them believe that sin is conquered when it has not been touched. By the time they have cooled, they must go back to the battle; and the lust they thought was slain turns out to have had no wound.

And if things are so sad with those who labor and strive and yet do not enter the kingdom, what is the condition of those who despise all this? They are continually under the power and rule of sin, and love to have it so, and are troubled at nothing except that they cannot make enough provision for the flesh, to fulfill its lusts.
